00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:06.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in europe  and around the world the coronavirus  pandemic has caused  unprecedented economic challenges  but as more europeans get vaccinated  and restrictions are lifted growth is  expected to bounce back  it should be fueled by private  consumption  recovering investment and stronger  demands for european  exports national support schemes  backed by the eu have helped to prevent  a surge in joblessness  although the increase in female and  youth unemployment has been more marked  while hiring activity looks subdued in  the near term  stronger economic growth should help  unemployment fall back towards  pre-crisis level inflation  rose early this year due to some  temporary factors notably energy prices  but the underlying trend remains low  and is forecast to be broadly stable  governments across the eu have had to  increase their borrowing  to finance public support for the  economy  this has caused deficits to rise  above 3 percent of gdp in almost  all member states depth levels  have also risen to over 100 percent of  gdp  in the euro area we are expected to  start decreasing again  in 2022  our economic recovery is closely linked  to our recovery from the pandemic  and while we don't know what long-term  scars covet  may leave on the economy we do know  that the eu countries are set to receive  a major boost  from next generation eu which will  increase  as more funds are invested  [Music]  the strength and the pace of the  recovery  will differ across the eu but our new  common policies  will prepare us for the economic and  social challenges of the future  building a more competitive green  and digital europe  [Music]  you

